#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9ceefc-3aa8-5c13-90a1-e4a084f720cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "1c04b787-534d-588e-8f53-55dd81ca5fd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14041" :question/problem "Sketch the solutions to the inequality $x+2y < 4$ in the $xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & <4 \\\\ 2y & <-x+4 \\\\ y & <-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny < -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1450257c533c670d3ce0d803aa62b75c2ffe44159e26a3daf5dd3c04026a6fe4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "031f0fd6-385d-56b8-9e8e-b264e6e89ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/id #uuid "845cd3b6-4a16-5501-b031-d57c4bb89457" :question/math-academy-id "e-14047" :question/problem "Sketch the solutions to the inequality $-x-y \\leq -1$ in the $xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-y & \\le -1 \\\\ -y & \\le x-1 \\\\ y & \\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-x+1.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-x+1$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a *solid line*.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny \\geq -x+1\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line $y=-x+1,$ so we shade *above* the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a649958285f52e9d61c665bf4cd58844e4ed7cea1cf9e0f949efe5a0f25e3263.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183888 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecbf45e4-67a6-58cd-9655-4a339de64db5" :knowledge-point/title "Inequalities Involving Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "dc1ba796-5508-5697-a7ac-2cad5c767ffa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14056" :question/problem "Find the inequality that describes the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/677e2257e2cb3a5706b7dc2d1088fc68ff12a2c39d976819d52f25fb2dfbe77d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation \n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. \n\n- Since the line is *dashed*, we will use a *strict* inequality.\n\n- Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx > -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "031f0fd6-385d-56b8-9e8e-b264e6e89ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17abc78a-785b-5192-8fa7-439ec6917908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/b1fc6cc7406d0f47b142949371b66c59aac6bcdf8bdc7dbc62a113e063642fe2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285065} {:answer/id #uuid "ce553c50-eee6-5849-a160-0b18133622f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/2ae1370dda61376a0c8b684a33f3722620aef05ee289f0a83591546e49221389.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285066} {:answer/id #uuid "26b3b76d-ea9a-56df-8296-b5a24c12c7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/419a5f107674817f111a0eed14a49cc91d007d592b61e2c0c5440967f607a356.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285067} {:answer/id #uuid "aecd5e66-a26f-5174-9950-637139443167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/2f186f14dde18a41004fd77e7280686f32bfc97c9c35f722e5c799c498a36a50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285068} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e86715-8636-5654-ba90-c6253250ec74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/bb3db9d0eaa602c87df225b21be99c0346c72ec142bb498b92671495bc77858a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26b3b76d-ea9a-56df-8296-b5a24c12c7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/419a5f107674817f111a0eed14a49cc91d007d592b61e2c0c5440967f607a356.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285067} :answer-field/id #uuid "73936b03-e1a1-55ee-8794-14b8b02ef2bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "187bfe25-9849-538d-9d2d-ce122bf58749" :question/math-academy-id "q-109940" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $-10x+5y\\ge -5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10x+5y & \\ge -5 \\\\ 5y & \\ge 10x-5 \\\\ y & \\ge 2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=2x-1.$ Since the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=2x-1$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a solid line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y\\ge 2x-1$ refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line $y=2x-1,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a9eef4f75cb108bd00fa1230a4be9f5b9edfc21d0ff7f999a3ca8aeb212f08b7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9ceefc-3aa8-5c13-90a1-e4a084f720cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac719246-6a3c-5b11-be2a-1ac4e31ae591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/0668624761edfaf6f67a69d6059e7b0c54acd87042ec9f7400f1d4c2dfc5ff0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285072} {:answer/id #uuid "239033c2-0a8b-51a0-b255-a4f2efa1928f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a16d99b349797e305083d8295ef098fbfbe3ea82970b5a48606ea5c678e08262.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285073} {:answer/id #uuid "1780ed63-0950-59cc-97d0-725d4da92b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/ebf7fd57f5d07afebc22899418ce34d14e59dcdb2a01087950de675b7086f405.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285074} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6eb7d3-0717-5007-b786-e41874f150b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1e408923db2c7223715b9f82a2777a411e2c79b3614320975c237f2f503f2368.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285075} {:answer/id #uuid "f0582487-dca4-5d7d-a854-862539ca2500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/458a5dd519867a662237d7b52e44627d1867f25dcceeea446203a42571f91a00.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "239033c2-0a8b-51a0-b255-a4f2efa1928f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a16d99b349797e305083d8295ef098fbfbe3ea82970b5a48606ea5c678e08262.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285073} :answer-field/id #uuid "88498f1b-dff4-5ff5-9e9f-1dfa6a2f609b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3626480d-f8b9-54d9-af65-fe6ef10bb1f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109942" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $5x-10y<-20?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-10y & <-20 \\\\ -10y & <-5x-20 \\\\ 10y & >5x+20 \\\\ y & >\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y>\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/351bb4b4324432e2cc747e67dac22100da3c9e5318339832efa046f504394c35.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "031f0fd6-385d-56b8-9e8e-b264e6e89ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a95ac4-5aa6-5ea5-a4d1-51498103bbf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a918cfee32950317ee7621317d5af40c49ca04209b981c6e94d529c8d7896384.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285079} {:answer/id #uuid "57547bda-e94c-5c99-b1b7-5cdac989b549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/8104d0507b16ac8c43900e8278e8d5079f99539b56c9eb150b938751d6c57810.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285080} {:answer/id #uuid "e63193d3-8bd2-538b-8da7-4ac2b4ecd2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/d63cc52412e63eb0658ffe92818fecefcc4a19e4cf9f3f7cad3b93de34dedb87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285081} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfb2952-20b8-5546-9431-f982d2e905b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/73bf01c051eadfb5c9b020147d7b2b4656ca1b5d3c1d8ffac0a58071d772ed9e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285082} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c154f6-233f-501f-b572-e870f1aea62a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/672613f47a02497d7247227981e8e654906f442c021d19e0d6f0180905520935.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a95ac4-5aa6-5ea5-a4d1-51498103bbf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a918cfee32950317ee7621317d5af40c49ca04209b981c6e94d529c8d7896384.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285079} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4845259-70e8-54d5-8fec-ae787e49366b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fc6e780-0a9e-5665-a2f1-8e50997d5b65" :question/math-academy-id "q-109946" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $2x-y\\ge 3?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & \\ge 3 \\\\ -y & \\ge -2x+3 \\\\ y & \\le 2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=2x-3.$ Since the inequality involves a 'less than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=2x-3$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a solid line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y\\le 2x-3$ refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line $y=2x-3,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/020b2d81ed5913dfe41fb296811da30ee2c6ea4fc7ea8184335db6d866598c5a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecbf45e4-67a6-58cd-9655-4a339de64db5" :knowledge-point/title "Inequalities Involving Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6cde624-1abe-5222-a795-2015958a5bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285086} {:answer/id #uuid "7c04131b-3cd3-51bf-98e9-c6c9c5b44a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285087} {:answer/id #uuid "cd0ddf99-d028-58e8-9620-c4a5673267c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285088} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a005d9-d31f-5bd6-8ad2-43275b1e6fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285089} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d464bd-1f89-5640-8681-20dd4707756c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6cde624-1abe-5222-a795-2015958a5bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285086} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ae532c-1348-5ebf-8172-fa85ca2be79a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1556706-2cb2-5ff8-b0a8-831a261163b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109949" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/feed76c05adc7d92f080edf65cc9b94a336d2833397e43c34beb350dc3be514e.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above." :question/worked-solution "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(3,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $x=3.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is to the *left* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *less than* those on the line $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx<3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9ceefc-3aa8-5c13-90a1-e4a084f720cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a46ce7eb-5748-585d-960b-3cb5b8654049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1497c291a9a068a414503d3081f52c5df56019d496a3f6c16cc905e6a20b97fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285093} {:answer/id #uuid "85fd2ac2-9e10-5d68-9347-cc87629137ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/9e6af7aceed6e31bccd5f568d50ac1c897b98cc568d383ea559a8b61abc203e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285094} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b1afc0-61aa-5f26-b113-daeb8d01ee71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1cc25938438d7ef2ad77bd5047a1417e5c0dba609e630bde7c3dee9dffa205ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285095} {:answer/id #uuid "33578693-938f-5119-8aff-8c8ab6acf599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285096} {:answer/id #uuid "c49089de-1331-503a-9c8a-66650785a75a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/9febb7b2ffb18d3d4d1ea5976fb0de6ee164689d01d82cf89dfb83000febffb1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33578693-938f-5119-8aff-8c8ab6acf599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/07de9cd8bb359093767ba2dfe9e762ca602d97dbe7b1217541fe4b5017675162.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285096} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0e79566-0f3e-5233-a3f1-b33af357af0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cee14fe-e375-5314-aab5-fe2b10bd1948" :question/math-academy-id "q-142097" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $4x-2y<6?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & <6 \\\\ -2y & <-4x+6 \\\\ 2y & >4x-6 \\\\ y & >2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=2x-3.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=2x-3$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny>2x-3\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=2x-3,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/2fe69dace2487c1b6dc73fcd122fd358513d8ac9528b3195d2075f8b62c75b4a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "031f0fd6-385d-56b8-9e8e-b264e6e89ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fi